---
import Callout from '../Callout.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s7">
  <div class="num">07</div>
  <h2>Staying safe</h2>
  <p class="lede">Four rules. They cover almost everything that goes wrong.</p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Never paste secrets into a prompt'}
      ><p>
        No passwords, no API keys (long codes that let a program use a paid
        service), no bank details, no one else's private information. If a tool
        needs a key, the docs will show a proper place to put it. A prompt is
        never that place.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Read the plan before you approve'}
      ><p>
        Every time. If a plan includes deleting things, installing things or
        touching files outside your project, stop and ask why.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Keep work in a project folder'}
      ><p>
        Open agents in <code class="i">my-site</code>, never in your home folder
        or your whole Documents. What the agent can see is what the agent can
        change.
      </p></StepCard
    >
    <StepCard number="4" title={'Make mistakes undoable'}
      ><p>
        Delta's separate copies mean a bad thread can be thrown away. Outside
        Delta, ask Claude Code to "set up Git and commit" before big changes.
        Git is a save history for a folder, and a commit is one saved snapshot
        you can go back to.
      </p></StepCard
    >
  </div>
  <Callout title={'If something feels off, stop'} warning
    ><svg
      slot="icon"
      width="22"
      height="22"
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      fill="none"
      stroke="var(--color-ffb347)"
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
      ><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path><path
        d="M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z"
      ></path></svg
    ><p>
      Reject the change, close the thread and message me. Nothing in a project
      folder is urgent enough to guess.
    </p></Callout
  >
  <div class="card" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px">
    <div class="num" style="color: var(--color-b8ffd0)">
      Why the asking matters
    </div>
    <p>
      An agent that is trying hard to finish will sometimes find a route you did
      not intend. It is not being sneaky. It has a goal, it hits a wall and it
      looks for a way around, and the way around might be something you would
      never have agreed to. The models you will use have a lot of guard rails,
      but this is the reason you keep the approval prompts on and keep the agent
      inside one folder. Every yes you give is a small decision that stays
      yours.
    </p>
    <p>
      My practical advice, which lines up with what people who run agents on
      their own work every day have found: start with tasks you already know
      well, so you can tell whether it did them right. Start with the strictest
      approval settings and loosen them only after the agent has earned it.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: var(--color-4f9a68)">
      Read: <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/ai-risk-plain-english"
        >AI risk in plain English</a
      > and <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/astra-review-everyday-work"
        >Using an agent for everyday work</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
